# CLI

Reference for the `wyrd` binary. It has four command groups: `auth`, `eval`,
`principal`, and `gateway`. The binary is built from the repo — see [Get started](/get-started/).

Every server-facing command reads `--server` from `WYRD_SERVER_URL` when the flag
is omitted. No command takes a secret as an argument: server-facing commands
read their credential from the ambient chain (`WYRD_ACCESS_TOKEN`, workload
identity, `WYRD_API_KEY`, a saved `wyrd auth login`, or
`~/.config/wyrd/credentials.toml`), so it never lands in shell history or the
process table. See [Client configuration](/get-started/client-configuration/#credentials).

## wyrd auth

Authenticate with a Wyrd server and manage credentials.

### auth login

Sign a person in to one tenant with OAuth 2.0 device authorization
([RFC 8628](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8628)) and save the login every
local SDK uses. The command prints a one-time code and a verification URL and
opens it in a browser; the person approves the code and signs in at the
tenant's IdP. No token is printed. Each completed login becomes the newest for its
server. Logging in again to the same tenant replaces its earlier login; logins
to other tenants are kept. Ctrl-C cancels.

```bash
wyrd auth login --server https://wyrd.example.com --tenant acme
```

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Flag', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Required' },
    { header: 'Env' },
    { header: 'Meaning' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['--server', 'yes', 'WYRD_SERVER_URL', 'Wyrd server base URL.'],
    ['--tenant', 'yes', 'WYRD_TENANT', 'Route key of the tenant to sign in to.'],
    ['--no-browser', 'no', '—', 'Print the verification URL without opening a browser.']
  ]}
/>

### auth logout

End a saved login: delete it locally, then revoke it on the server
best-effort ([RFC 7009](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7009)). A failed
revocation prints a warning; the login is already gone locally. An access
token already issued stays valid until it expires, at most five minutes.
Logout does not end the IdP session.

```bash
wyrd auth logout --server https://wyrd.example.com --tenant acme
```

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Flag', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Required' },
    { header: 'Env' },
    { header: 'Meaning' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['--server', 'yes', 'WYRD_SERVER_URL', 'Wyrd server base URL of the login to end.'],
    ['--tenant', 'no', 'WYRD_TENANT', 'Tenant of the login to end. Defaults to the newest login for the server.']
  ]}
/>

### auth status

List saved logins (server, tenant, and access-token expiry) without printing
any token.

```bash
wyrd auth status
```

### auth refresh

Rotate a human session's Wyrd refresh token and print the new access and
refresh tokens. Only a `User` principal holds one; a machine renews by
re-exchanging its API key instead. The refresh token is read only from
`WYRD_REFRESH_TOKEN`.

```bash
WYRD_REFRESH_TOKEN="$TOKEN" wyrd auth refresh --server https://acme.wyrd.cloud
```

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Flag', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Required' },
    { header: 'Env' },
    { header: 'Meaning' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['--server', 'yes', 'WYRD_SERVER_URL', 'Wyrd server base URL.']
  ]}
/>

### auth issue-key

Issue an API key bound to a card ref (`POST /auth/issue-key`). Requires an admin
credential in the ambient chain. Prints the key once, with the `principal_id`
that [`wyrd principal role`](#wyrd-principal-role) takes.

```bash
wyrd auth issue-key \
  --kind service --name checkout --version 1.0.0 --space prod \
  --server https://acme.wyrd.cloud
```

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Flag', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Required' },
    { header: 'Env' },
    { header: 'Meaning' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['--kind', 'yes', '—', 'Card kind the key is bound to (e.g. service, agent).'],
    ['--name', 'yes', '—', 'Card name.'],
    ['--version', 'yes', '—', 'Card version (e.g. 1.0.0).'],
    ['--space', 'yes', '—', 'Card space.'],
    ['--label', 'no', '—', 'Label stored with the key row.'],
    ['--expires-in-seconds', 'no', '—', 'TTL override in seconds.'],
    ['--server', 'yes', 'WYRD_SERVER_URL', 'Wyrd server base URL.']
  ]}
/>

## wyrd eval run

Score pre-collected observations locally against an Eval-backed Verifier card
(`kind: Verifier` with `implementation.kind: eval`). The command reads a local
card file and a JSONL of records; it does not contact a server.

```bash
wyrd eval run --eval ./verifier.yaml --records ./observations.jsonl \
  --subject support/Agent/triage@1.0.0
```

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Flag', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Default' },
    { header: 'Meaning' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['--eval', '(required)', 'Eval-backed Verifier card filesystem path (JSON or YAML).'],
    ['--records', '(required)', 'JSONL of pre-collected EvalRecordObservation rows.'],
    ['--subject', '(required)', 'CardRef of the Card that emitted the records.'],
    ['--judge-mock', 'false', 'Deterministic judge — all LLM judge tasks return passed: true. Required if the spec has LLM judges and no provider is configured.'],
    ['--baseline', '—', 'Baseline EvalResults JSON for comparison.'],
    ['--out', '—', 'Directory for local output artifacts (env WYRD_EVAL_OUT).'],
    ['--fail-on-gate', 'true', 'Exit non-zero when the local pass gate fails.']
  ]}
/>

## wyrd principal revoke

Suspend a principal so it can mint no new token; tokens it already holds lapse
within five minutes. Requires an admin credential in the ambient chain. The
principal ID is positional; `--kind` is required because IDs are unique only
within a kind.

```bash
wyrd principal revoke 3f9a...uuid --kind service --reason "key leaked" \
  --server https://acme.wyrd.cloud
```

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Argument', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Required' },
    { header: 'Env' },
    { header: 'Meaning' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['<id>', 'yes', '—', 'Principal ID (UUID) to revoke.'],
    ['--kind', 'yes', '—', 'Principal kind: user, service, or agent.'],
    ['--reason', 'yes', '—', 'Audit reason for the revocation.'],
    ['--server', 'yes', 'WYRD_SERVER_URL', 'Wyrd server base URL.']
  ]}
/>

## wyrd principal list

Page through the tenant's assignable users and Service and Agent principals
(`GET /v1/principals`), printing each id, kind, and name, then `next: <id>`
when another page follows. Filters are exact. Requires an admin credential in
the ambient chain.

```bash
wyrd principal list --kind service --name checkout --server https://acme.wyrd.cloud
```

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Flag', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Required' },
    { header: 'Env' },
    { header: 'Meaning' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['--kind', 'no', '—', 'Only principals of this kind: user, service, or agent.'],
    ['--email', 'no', '—', 'Only the user with this exact email.'],
    ['--name', 'no', '—', 'Only the service or agent with this exact name.'],
    ['--limit', 'no', '—', 'Page size, 1 to 200 (default 100).'],
    ['--after', 'no', '—', 'Continue after this principal id, the previous page\'s next.'],
    ['--server', 'yes', 'WYRD_SERVER_URL', 'Wyrd server base URL.']
  ]}
/>

## wyrd principal role

List a principal's Roles with their source (`list`), or grant (`grant`) or
revoke (`revoke`) a direct Role (`PUT` and `DELETE
/v1/principals/{id}/roles/{role}`). Grant and revoke are idempotent and print
whether anything changed and the resulting assignments; they require a tenant
administrator (`*`) credential in the ambient chain. Revoking leaves a user's
`idp` assignment of the same Role to the identity provider. A change reaches
the principal's next token. See [Authorization](/concepts/authorization/).

```bash
wyrd principal role grant --principal 3f9a...uuid --role editor \
  --server https://acme.wyrd.cloud
```

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Flag', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Required' },
    { header: 'Env' },
    { header: 'Meaning' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['--principal', 'yes', '—', 'Principal ID (UUID) to act on.'],
    ['--role', 'grant, revoke', '—', 'Built-in or tenant Role (e.g. editor).'],
    ['--server', 'yes', 'WYRD_SERVER_URL', 'Wyrd server base URL.']
  ]}
/>

## wyrd gateway

Administer tenant gateway resources. Every verb prints the server's JSON
response on stdout (deletes print nothing) and failures print the originating
problem on stderr. See [Administer the gateway](/how-to/administer-the-gateway/)
for lifecycle rules.

```bash
wyrd gateway credential put --file credential.json
WYRD_ACCESS_TOKEN=... wyrd gateway deployment list --server https://acme.wyrd.cloud
```

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Command', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Meaning' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['credential put --file <path>', 'Create or rotate the credential named in the document.'],
    ['credential get <name> | list', 'Read redacted credentials.'],
    ['credential revoke <name>', 'Terminally revoke a credential.'],
    ['credential delete <name>', 'Delete an unreferenced credential; absent names succeed.'],
    ['deployment put --file <path> | get <name> | list | delete <name>', 'Manage provider deployments.'],
    ['fallback-policy put --file <path> | get | delete', 'Replace, read, or reset the fallback policy.'],
    ['governance-policy put --file <path> | get | delete', 'Replace, read, or reset the governance policy.'],
    ['capture-policy put --file <path> | get', 'Replace or read the capture policy.'],
    ['--server', 'Wyrd server base URL (WYRD_SERVER_URL). Credentials resolve from WYRD_ACCESS_TOKEN, WYRD_API_KEY, or client configuration; the CLI never accepts them as arguments.']
  ]}
/>

## wyrd operator-connection

Manage the tenant's encrypted [Operator connections](/how-to/operate/operator-connections/).
`create` and `update` read the whole request body, secret included, from a JSON
or YAML file, or from stdin with `--body-file -`, so a credential never appears
in argv or shell history. Every verb prints the server's redacted JSON view.
Reads require `operators:read`; writes require `operators:write`.

```bash
wyrd operator-connection create --body-file slack.yaml --server https://acme.wyrd.cloud
wyrd operator-connection list --server https://acme.wyrd.cloud
```

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Command', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Meaning' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['create --body-file <path>', 'Create a connection from a provider-tagged body.'],
    ['list', 'List every connection in the tenant, redacted.'],
    ['get --connection-id <uuid>', 'Read one connection, redacted.'],
    ['update --connection-id <uuid> --body-file <path>', 'Patch a connection: rotate its secret, change nonsecret configuration, or set status to active or disabled.'],
    ['disable --connection-id <uuid>', 'Disable a connection. It is retained, never deleted; re-enable it with update.'],
    ['--server', 'Wyrd server base URL (WYRD_SERVER_URL). The credential resolves from the ambient chain.']
  ]}
/>

## wyrd mcp

Connect local MCP hosts to Wyrd. `install` writes a launch entry for
`wyrd mcp proxy` into each selected host's user configuration; the host then
runs the proxy, which serves the server's own `/mcp` tools over stdio and
authenticates every request through the ambient credential chain. Host files
never hold a credential.

```bash
wyrd mcp install --host claude-code --host codex
wyrd mcp install --host cursor --server https://acme.wyrd.cloud
```

<DataTable
  columns={[
    { header: 'Command', role: 'name' },
    { header: 'Meaning' }
  ]}
  rows={[
    ['install', 'On a terminal, offer the detected hosts as a multi-select. Without a terminal, --host is required and nothing is configured implicitly.'],
    ['install --host <host>', 'Connect one host; repeat for several: codex, claude-code, copilot-cli, vscode, cursor, pi, hermes. Each host is reported as added, updated, or unchanged, or with why its file was left as it was.'],
    ['install --server <url>', 'Keep this server in the host entry. The global config.toml endpoint is not changed. Omitted, the proxy follows the global endpoint.'],
    ['proxy [--server <url>]', 'Serve Wyrd\'s MCP tools to a host over stdio. Hosts launch this; you rarely run it directly.']
  ]}
/>

Each entry pins `WYRD_CONFIG_HOME`, so hosts that pass a reduced environment
still find your saved login or `credentials.toml`. An existing `wyrd` entry
that does not run the proxy is a conflict: rename or remove it, then rerun.
Failures use the [MCP host codes](/api/errors/#mcp-host-codes).

